  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 1, $j$1.1 Key Elements and Layers of Meaning$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Ruth 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ruth/1"}], "body": "A key element is a building block of a story or poem: setting, character, conflict, plot, point of view, imagery, symbol, tone and word choice. Strong readers notice how these elements work together to create layers of meaning.\n\nLayer one is the literal meaning, what the words directly say. Layer two is the deeper meaning, what the details suggest about people, feelings or life.\n\nRead this original passage:\n\nThe porch light at the Carter house had been burning for three nights. Nobody had changed the bulb, though it flickered like a tired heartbeat. Inside, Mrs. Carter kept a place set at the table for a son who had said he would be home by Sunday. Each evening she washed the extra plate and put it back.\n\nLiteral meaning: a family is waiting for someone to come home. Deeper meaning: the light and the plate are symbols of hope and faithful love, even when waiting is painful.\n\nYour task: (1) Name two key elements in the passage and quote one detail for each. (2) Write one sentence for the literal meaning and one sentence for a deeper layer. (3) Explain in two sentences how one detail supports your deeper layer.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.R.1.1, ELA.K12.EE.1.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.R.1.1, ELA.K12.EE.1.1", "script": "Welcome to English 2. I am Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr., and I am glad you are here. Listen, a good reader is a good detective. Today we learn to notice the small details that carry big meaning.\n\nLet us begin with key elements. Every story or poem is built from parts. The setting is where and when it happens. The characters are the people or voices. The conflict is the struggle. The plot is the order of events. Point of view is who is telling it. Imagery is language that appeals to the senses. A symbol is an object or detail that stands for a larger idea. Tone is the attitude of the writing. And word choice, which we call diction, is the specific words the author selects.\n\nNow watch this. Elements do not work alone. They work together to build layers of meaning. The first layer is literal. It is what the words directly say. The second layer is deeper. It is what the details suggest about people, feelings or life.\n\nLook at our sample passage. A porch light has been burning for three nights. A mother sets a place at the table and washes the same plate again each evening. On the literal level, a family is waiting for a son to come home. But why does the author tell us the light flickers like a tired heartbeat? Why does she wash a plate nobody uses? Those details are symbols. The light stands for hope. The plate stands for faithful love that refuses to give up. The deeper layer says that love keeps watch even when waiting hurts.\n\nHere is how to prove a deeper meaning. Use three steps. First, notice the detail. Second, ask what it might stand for. Third, explain how the detail supports your idea, using words from the text. Always cite evidence. A claim without evidence is only an opinion.\n\nOne caution. Do not force a symbol where none exists. Sometimes a lamp is only a lamp. Look for repetition, emphasis and strong feeling. When an author repeats a detail, the author wants you to notice it.\n\nYour task today has three parts. Name two key elements and quote a detail for each. Write one sentence for the literal meaning and one for a deeper layer. Then explain how one detail supports your idea. After that, take the four question check.\n\nReading closely is a gift. It teaches us to see what others miss. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.1 Key Elements and Layers of Meaning$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 2, $j$1.2 Theme and How It Develops$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Luke 15 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Luke/15"}], "body": "A theme is the message about life or human experience that a text communicates. A topic is a subject, such as forgiveness. A theme is a full statement about that subject, such as: Forgiveness restores what anger breaks. State a theme in a complete sentence, not in one word.\n\nA universal theme speaks to people in every time and culture, such as courage, grace, loyalty and justice. Themes develop as a story moves. Watch how the main character's choices, the conflict and its outcome, and repeated images build the message.\n\nRead Luke 15:11 to 32 in the King James Version, the parable of the prodigal son. The younger son wastes his inheritance and returns in shame. In verse 20 his father \"saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him.\" In verse 24 the father says, \"For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found.\"\n\nYour task: (1) Write a theme statement for this parable in one complete sentence. (2) Give two places in the story where the theme develops and explain each in one or two sentences. (3) Explain the difference between a topic and a theme.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.R.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.1.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.R.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.1.1", "script": "Listen, one of the most important skills in reading is finding the theme. Today we learn how to state it, how to prove it, and how it grows.\n\nFirst, topic versus theme. A topic is a subject, like forgiveness, courage or pride. A theme is the message the text sends about that subject. So forgiveness is a topic, but Forgiveness restores what anger breaks is a theme. A theme is always a full sentence, and it is something a reader can think about and discuss.\n\nNow, universal themes. A universal theme is one that speaks to people in every time and place. Loyalty, justice, grace, courage and hope are universal. That is why a story from long ago can still move us today. The human heart has not changed.\n\nWatch this. Themes do not appear all at once. They develop. An author builds a theme through the choices a character makes, through the conflict and how it ends, and through images or events that repeat. When you track those moments, you can see the message grow.\n\nLet us look at the parable of the prodigal son in Luke chapter fifteen, in the King James Version. The younger son asks for his inheritance and wastes it. When he has nothing, he decides to go home. Here is the turning point. While he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran. The father does not wait for an apology. He runs. Then the father says, For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found.\n\nNow state a theme. One strong choice is: Grace welcomes the one who returns. Notice it is a complete sentence and it says something about life. Now prove it. First, the son's failure and need set up the conflict. Second, the father's running and embrace answer the conflict with grace. Third, the father's words about being lost and found repeat the idea of restoration. Those three moments develop the theme.\n\nYour task today. Write a theme statement for this parable in one sentence. Give two places where the theme develops and explain each. Then explain the difference between a topic and a theme. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nA good reader does not only ask what happened. A good reader asks what it means. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.2 Theme and How It Develops$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 3, $j$1.3 Coming of Age and Conflicting Perspectives$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "1 Samuel 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/1Samuel/3"}], "body": "A coming of age story follows a young character who moves from innocence toward maturity through an important experience. The character often learns something about responsibility, identity or faith.\n\nAuthors also show conflicting perspectives, which means different characters see the same situation in different ways. Good readers understand each view and notice how the author represents them.\n\nScripture offers an example. \"And the child Samuel ministered unto the LORD before Eli\" (1 Samuel 3:1). As Samuel hears the call of God and answers, \"Speak; for thy servant heareth\" (1 Samuel 3:10), he steps into new responsibility. Later we read that \"Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him\" (1 Samuel 3:19).\n\nRead this original passage:\n\nDani had circled the date on the calendar for months: Saturday, the regional science fair. But Saturday was also the day her father opened the hardware store, and he had counted on her at the register since she was twelve. \"You are needed here,\" he said. Everyone needs something from me, she thought, and then felt ashamed, because the store had paid for every book on her shelf. That night she asked to speak with him, not to argue, but to listen first.\n\nYour task: (1) State Dani's perspective and her father's perspective in one sentence each. (2) Explain what Dani learns by the end of the passage and the detail that shows it. (3) Name one way Samuel's story is also a coming of age story.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.R.1.3, ELA.K12.EE.3.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.R.1.3, ELA.K12.EE.3.1", "script": "Now, let us talk about growing up in literature. Authors have written about it for thousands of years, because every one of us goes through it.\n\nA coming of age story follows a young character who moves from innocence toward maturity. Something happens that tests the character. A decision must be made. A loss is faced. A responsibility is accepted. By the end, the character sees the world, and himself or herself, in a new way.\n\nLook at the Bible. The child Samuel ministered unto the LORD before Eli. One night he hears his name called, and he does not yet know it is God. Eli helps him understand, and Samuel answers, Speak; for thy servant heareth. That is a young person stepping into new responsibility with humility. The chapter ends by telling us that Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him. Growth is the heart of every coming of age story.\n\nNext, conflicting perspectives. This means different characters see the same situation differently. Neither view has to be wicked. Often both come from something good. In our passage, Dani wants the science fair because she wants to learn and grow. Her father wants her at the store because he values family, work and dependability. Both views matter to the story.\n\nWatch how the author represents these views. We hear the father speak directly, You are needed here. We hear Dani's private thought, Everyone needs something from me. Then comes the turning point. She feels ashamed, remembering that the store paid for every book on her shelf. That shows maturity, because she can now see her father's view as well as her own.\n\nHere is a skill for you. When you read, make an inference. An inference is a conclusion based on evidence plus what you already know. The author never says Dani grows up. But when she chooses to listen first, we infer that she is learning patience and respect.\n\nYour task today. State each perspective in one sentence. Explain what Dani learns and cite the detail that shows it. Then tell how Samuel's story is also a coming of age story. When you finish, take the four question check.\n\nGrowing up is not only getting older. It is learning to see more clearly. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.3 Coming of Age and Conflicting Perspectives$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 4, $j$1.4 Reading Poetry for Layers of Meaning$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Psalm 23 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Psalms/23"}], "body": "A poem packs meaning into few words. Key terms: the speaker is the voice that talks; a line is one row of words; a stanza is a group of lines; imagery is language that appeals to the senses. Ambiguity means a word, image or line can reasonably mean more than one thing. Authors use ambiguity on purpose to create several layers of meaning.\n\nRead this original poem:\n\nEvening Field\nThe fence leans toward the road,\nand the road leans toward the town,\nand I stand between them\nholding the gate that will not close.\n\nRead Psalm 23 in the King James Version. David uses images such as \"green pastures\" and \"the still waters\" and \"the valley of the shadow of death.\" Each image carries more than one layer: rest, guidance, danger and comfort.\n\nYour task: (1) Describe the speaker of Evening Field in one sentence. (2) Name one image and the feeling it creates. (3) Give two possible meanings for \"the gate that will not close.\" (4) Tell which meaning you find stronger and why.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.R.1.4, ELA.K12.EE.3.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.R.1.4, ELA.K12.EE.3.1", "script": "Listen, poetry is the art of saying a lot with a little. Today we learn how to read a poem slowly and discover its layers.\n\nLet us start with the vocabulary. The speaker is the voice in the poem. It is not always the poet. A line is one row of words. A stanza is a group of lines, like a paragraph. Imagery is language that appeals to the senses, to sight, sound, touch, taste and smell.\n\nNow the big idea of this lesson: ambiguity. Ambiguity means a word, an image or a line can reasonably mean more than one thing. In a poem, that is a gift, not a mistake. Poets choose words that open doors.\n\nWatch this. Here is a short poem I wrote for our class. Evening Field. The fence leans toward the road, and the road leans toward the town, and I stand between them holding the gate that will not close.\n\nWho is the speaker? Someone standing in a field at evening, between the road and the town. What are the images? A leaning fence, a road, a gate. What is the mood? Quiet, a little uncertain.\n\nNow the last line. The gate that will not close. What could it mean? One layer says the speaker cannot finish a decision. The gate stays open because a choice has not been made. Another layer says the speaker is keeping a way open for someone who may return. Both readings fit the poem. Neither cancels the other. That is ambiguity at work.\n\nLook at the way scripture uses the same art. In Psalm 23, in the King James Version, David writes, He maketh me to lie down in green pastures: he leadeth me beside the still waters. These are pictures of rest and guidance. Then he writes, Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for thou art with me. Now the images carry danger and comfort together. The same poem holds peace and trouble, and the speaker trusts God in both.\n\nHere is how to read any poem. First, read it aloud. Second, name the speaker and the images. Third, ask what each image might stand for. Fourth, look for more than one possible meaning and choose the one the words support best.\n\nYour task today. Describe the speaker of Evening Field. Name one image and the feeling it creates. Give two meanings for the gate that will not close, and tell which you find stronger and why. Then take the four question check.\n\nSlow reading is how we hear what a poem is really saying. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.4 Reading Poetry for Layers of Meaning$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 1, $j$Assessment 1: Literary Analysis Paragraph$j$, $j${"prompt": "Write one literary analysis paragraph of 8 to 10 sentences. Choose one text: Luke 15:11 to 32 (King James Version), the Carter passage from Lesson 1.1, or the Dani passage from Lesson 1.3.\n\nYour paragraph must (1) begin with a topic sentence that states a theme in one complete sentence, (2) include at least two pieces of evidence taken directly from the text, (3) explain how each piece of evidence supports the theme, (4) name at least one key element such as symbol, conflict or point of view, and (5) end with a concluding sentence. Use complete sentences and correct grammar, and write in your own words.", "rubric": "100 points. Theme statement is a complete sentence and fits the text: 20 points. Evidence is specific and taken directly from the text: 30 points. Reasoning explains how the evidence proves the theme: 30 points. Organization, key element named, and conventions: 20 points. A score of 70 or above is passing."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 1: Literary Analysis Paragraph$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 1, $j$Discussion 1: A Story That Shaped Me$j$, $j${"prompt": "Think of a story, film, song or Bible account that changed the way you see life. Write a post of at least 120 words that (1) names the story, (2) states its theme in one complete sentence, and (3) gives one detail from the story that shows the theme. Then write a respectful reply of at least 40 words to another student's post, or answer this question: What would someone who sees the story differently say? Keep your tone kind and your language clean."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 1: A Story That Shaped Me$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 1, $j$Check 1.1: Key Elements and Layers of Meaning$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A symbol in a story is:", "options": ["An object or detail that stands for a larger idea", "A rule of grammar", "Only a character's name", "A summary of the plot"]}, {"q": "In the Carter passage, the burning porch light most likely suggests:", "options": ["A summer holiday", "A broken lamp", "Hope that someone will return", "The cost of electricity"]}, {"q": "Evidence from a text should be:", "options": ["A guess", "A specific detail that supports your point", "A detail from a different story", "Your opinion only"]}, {"q": "The literal meaning of a passage is:", "options": ["A hidden symbol only", "The author's biography", "What the words directly say", "The title"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.1: Key Elements and Layers of Meaning$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,2,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$A symbol stands for a larger idea.$j$,$j$The light stands for hope and watchful love.$j$,$j$Evidence is a specific detail from the text.$j$,$j$Literal means what the words directly say.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.1: Key Elements and Layers of Meaning$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 2, $j$Check 1.2: Theme and How It Develops$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Which of these is a theme statement?", "options": ["Chapter fifteen", "A story about a family", "Forgiveness", "Forgiveness restores what anger breaks."]}, {"q": "A theme differs from a topic because a theme:", "options": ["Is the title", "Makes a full statement about the subject", "Is the setting", "Is always one word"]}, {"q": "A universal theme is one that:", "options": ["Is found only in poems", "Has no evidence", "Appears in only one country", "Speaks to people across times and cultures"]}, {"q": "Which best shows how a theme develops?", "options": ["Changing the font", "Tracking how choices and events build the message", "Counting the pages", "Listing the authors"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.2: Theme and How It Develops$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,1,3,1}'::int[], array[$j$A theme is a complete sentence about life.$j$,$j$A theme says something about the topic.$j$,$j$Universal themes reach every time and place.$j$,$j$Themes develop through choices, conflict and repeated images.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.2: Theme and How It Develops$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 3, $j$Check 1.3: Coming of Age and Conflicting Perspectives$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A coming of age story mainly shows a character who:", "options": ["Moves toward maturity through an experience", "Only travels to another country", "Has no conflict", "Never changes"]}, {"q": "Conflicting perspectives means:", "options": ["The author is confused", "Characters see the same situation differently", "Two stories share a title", "The setting changes"]}, {"q": "An inference is:", "options": ["A conclusion based on evidence and what you know", "A list of characters", "A direct quotation only", "A wild guess"]}, {"q": "What detail shows Dani is maturing?", "options": ["She decides to listen first before arguing", "She closes the store", "She buys a new book", "She ignores her father"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.3: Coming of Age and Conflicting Perspectives$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,1,0,0}'::int[], array[$j$Coming of age means growth toward maturity.$j$,$j$Different characters see the situation in different ways.$j$,$j$Inferences rest on evidence.$j$,$j$Listening first shows patience and respect.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.3: Coming of Age and Conflicting Perspectives$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 4, $j$Check 1.4: Reading Poetry for Layers of Meaning$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "In poetry, ambiguity means:", "options": ["The poem rhymes", "The poem has a mistake", "The poem has no speaker", "A line or image can reasonably mean more than one thing"]}, {"q": "The speaker of a poem is:", "options": ["The voice that talks in the poem", "The last stanza", "The title", "Always the poet's family"]}, {"q": "A stanza is:", "options": ["A group of lines in a poem", "A type of rhyme only", "One word", "The title"]}, {"q": "Imagery is:", "options": ["A list of facts", "A footnote", "The poet's address", "Language that appeals to the senses"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.4: Reading Poetry for Layers of Meaning$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,0,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$Ambiguity opens more than one meaning.$j$,$j$The speaker is the voice in the poem.$j$,$j$A stanza is a group of lines.$j$,$j$Imagery creates pictures and sensations.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.4: Reading Poetry for Layers of Meaning$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 5, $j$Unit Quiz 1: Reading Literature$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A key element of a text is:", "options": ["The date it was printed", "The size of the book", "The author's age", "A building block such as setting, conflict or imagery"]}, {"q": "The literal meaning of a passage is:", "options": ["The theme only", "What the words directly say", "A guess about hidden symbols", "The title"]}, {"q": "Which sentence states a theme?", "options": ["Grace welcomes the one who returns.", "The prodigal son", "A parable in Luke", "Grace"]}, {"q": "A universal theme:", "options": ["Cannot be proven", "Speaks to people across times and cultures", "Applies to one time only", "Appears only in poems"]}, {"q": "In a coming of age story, the main character usually:", "options": ["Stays the same", "Leaves the story early", "Grows toward maturity through an experience", "Has no choices"]}, {"q": "Conflicting perspectives means:", "options": ["The poem is long", "The setting is unclear", "The author used two fonts", "Characters see the same situation differently"]}, {"q": "Ambiguity in a poem means:", "options": ["Words or images can reasonably mean more than one thing", "The poem has no speaker", "The poem has no meaning", "The poem is written in code"]}, {"q": "Strong evidence from a text is:", "options": ["A personal opinion", "A guess", "A summary of another story", "A specific detail you quote or point to"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 1: Reading Literature$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,1,0,1,2,3,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$Key elements are the building blocks of a text.$j$,$j$Literal means what the words directly say.$j$,$j$A theme is a complete sentence about life.$j$,$j$Universal themes reach every time and place.$j$,$j$The character grows toward maturity.$j$,$j$Characters can view the same event in different ways.$j$,$j$Ambiguity creates layers of meaning.$j$,$j$Evidence is a specific detail from the text.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 1: Reading Literature$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
